<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Editor.Blockout</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            The blockout toolset's editor half: the viewport mode that owns the grey-boxing gestures,
            its sub-object element modes, its toolbar and its claim on the keys every modelling tool
            binds. The mesh kernel it will edit is Core/Vixen.Geometry and is not this.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>editor;blockout;level-design;mode;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            IEditorMode, the command registry and the keymap: the shell's vocabulary, which is what a
            mode is expressed in.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.Ui\Vixen.Editor.Ui.csproj" />

        <!--
            The viewport, which arrived with docs/plan/24 § P2. Until the mode owned nothing but its
            keys it needed no pane; a mode that selects faces needs one to ask what is under the
            pointer, so it implements IViewportInput as well as IEditorMode and the application hands
            it the panes rather than adapting between the two. Both interfaces still live where they
            did, and the assemblies that declare them still do not reference each other.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.SceneView\Vixen.Editor.SceneView.csproj" />

        <!--
            The plugin contract, because this assembly registers itself rather than being
            registered. Doc 36 § F2 named Blockout twice as the feature that "is not a plugin; it
            is a project reference" — `BlockoutModule` is what makes the second half of that
            sentence a fact about the build rather than about the code.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.Plugin\Vixen.Editor.Plugin.csproj" />

        <!--
            ⚠ The first reference this assembly names outside Editor/, and it is a decision rather
            than an oversight being corrected.

            Everything above arrives through a sibling: `Vixen.Geometry` — the mesh kernel the
            Description calls "not this" — is transitive through Vixen.Editor.SceneView, and that
            arrangement is doc 24 § D1's, because the kernel is Core/ and the copies into MeshData
            and into the scene file belong beside the code that draws and saves them.

            The remesher is different in kind. It is not a copy or an adapter; it is a verb this mode
            offers — docs/plan/41 § D16 gives Blockout its own row, "a Retopologize verb, one undo
            entry, result selected", and names a boolean result as its natural input, which is
            geometry only this assembly produces. There is no seam to route it through: an interface
            like IMeshBaker exists because importing an asset is the asset database's job and this
            assembly must not do it, whereas remeshing a mesh is arithmetic over triangles with no
            device, no model and no assets, which is exactly what makes Vixen.Geometry.Remeshing a
            Core/ assembly rather than an Editor/ one. Naming it directly is what the layering
            permits and what the plan asks for; hiding it behind an abstraction would be ceremony
            around a pure function.

            It brings Vixen.Geometry.Uv with it — the remesher's packer, docs/plan/42 § D2 — which
            BlockoutUvPanel then uses for the same reason and would otherwise have had to name
            itself.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Geometry.Remeshing\Vixen.Geometry.Remeshing.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Editor.Blockout.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
